1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
A marketplace adding Pay with USDC
needs more than a wallet button.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
It needs payment terms, secure handoff, Arc USDC,
escrow, programmable payouts, and reconciliation.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Here’s the marketplace view:
exactly 0.05 USDC.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Pay with USDC moves to Settle’s planning surface
without creating an onchain order.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:44,500
The backend sends buyer, amount, deadlines,
and a 90 / 10 split to POST /api/v1/orders/plan.

6
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:55,000
This is explicitly non-mutating:
no chain, Circle, or database mutation.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Settle returns an order ID, terms hash,
exact amount, and normalized settlement terms.

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:15,000
executionAvailable is false.
This plan is not an onchain order.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,500
Hosted checkout carries canonical context:
0.05 USDC on Arc Testnet with a 90 / 10 split.

10
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:36,000
The QR contains only the hosted-checkout URL.
The customer’s wallet authorizes Arc USDC.

11
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The monorepo separates web, shared primitives,
server-only Circle code, contracts, and docs.

12
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The Circle SDK client and validated configuration
live behind a server-only boundary.

13
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Circle credentials and custody never enter browser code.
Contract Platform tooling lives here too.

14
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Settle validates the funded order
and prepares the exact releaseOrder call.

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Settle decides. Circle’s controlled wallet executes.
Circle does not decide settlement policy.

16
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:30,000
fundOrder accepts only the stored buyer
and transfers the exact Arc USDC amount.

17
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,000
A balance check rejects transfer mismatch.
The contract is the canonical escrow boundary.

18
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,000
releaseOrder distributes the full amount
using stored basis-point terms.

19
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Refund and dispute paths preserve
explicit marketplace lifecycle semantics.

20
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Buyer, Arc chain, allowance, canonical state,
and readiness are checked before approve or fund.

21
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Final testing surfaced a gating edge case.
The fix and regression coverage fail closed safely.

22
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
85 web tests pass.
167 Foundry contract tests pass.

23
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The immutable order is Completed.
The 0.05 USDC is no longer active escrow.

24
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The split paid 0.045 USDC to the merchant
and 0.005 USDC to the platform.

25
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,500
ArcScan shows the real release transaction
against the verified SettlementEscrow contract.

26
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:02,000
Transfer logs match both recipients.
The release used Settle’s Circle-controlled path.

27
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:12,000
The contract record confirms verified source,
Arc context, and the canonical settlement boundary.

28
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:21,000
The docs cover quickstart, API reference,
checkout, lifecycle, settlement, and Arc configuration.

29
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Current evidence is USDC, Developer-Controlled Wallets,
and Circle Contract Platform infrastructure.

30
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Settle connects Payment Intent, wallet-authorized Arc USDC,
canonical escrow, and programmable settlement.

31
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Circle infrastructure stays at the controlled server boundary.
This is payment infrastructure, not just escrow.
